and possibly an override later:

```text
preferred editor = ...
```

if a particular project genuinely needs one.

This keeps projects portable when you change software.

Also keep your existing `apps` concept semantically separate:

> `apps` = applications/session state remembered and restored with the project.

It should not become:

> `apps` = the three buttons shown on the project tile.

Those are different concepts.

---

# I would adjust the meaning of “open project”

Right now Projects already has an `Open` operation that restores remembered applications.

That's useful, but Home needs a weaker operation too.

There are really three actions:

```text
Enter Project
    → make it the current project
    → launch nothing

Launch Tool
    → terminal / files / editor
    → scoped to project

Restore Workspace
    → reopen remembered project applications
```

You already have `SetCurrent`, so **Enter Project** can basically exist today without changing the protocol.

That makes a project tile much less aggressive.

Sometimes you just want:

```text
LEGE
    → Terminal
```

You shouldn't have to resurrect six old windows first.

---

# Home should become less of a file manager

This is the bigger recommendation.

Your existing Home implementation has started embedding actual file browsing into Downloads/Documents/Media/Drives.

Given your reaction to PCManFM versus the current Files UI, I would pull back from that.

Use the **small-widget → full-application** principle instead.

For example:

```text
┌ Downloads ──────────────┐
│ 3 recent                 │
│ paper.pdf                │
│ archive.zip              │
│ screenshot.png           │
│                          │
│ Open Downloads →         │
└──────────────────────────┘
```

That is useful.

Turning the expanded widget into another full hierarchical file manager is mostly duplication.

Likewise:

### Documents

Home:

```text
recent / pinned / relevant documents
```

Full navigation:

```text
PCManFM
```

### Drives

Home:

```text
2.0 TB
73% used
health good
```

Click:

```text
drive root in PCManFM
```

### Downloads

Home:

```text
recent files + active transfers
```

Click:

```text
full directory
```

### Projects

Home:

```text
semantic project controls
```

Click Files:

```text
project root in PCManFM
```

That is a much stronger division of labor.

---

# The taskbar/Home split is also correct

I would now remove **ordinary running-window management from Home**.

Your earlier Home design included concepts such as parked windows and the Window Shelf. With a dedicated taskbar being reworked, that distinction is no longer necessary.

Make:

```text
Taskbar = what is running now

Home = what matters across time
```

That is very clean.

A project tile can still show something understated like:

```text
LEGE
3 running
```

because that's useful context.

But clicking those three running windows or selecting individual ones belongs to the taskbar.

This avoids the classic desktop-shell problem where task switching, workspace management, launching and file organization slowly converge into the same enormous interface.

---

# A project widget should scale by available space

This also fits your existing representation-ladder idea unusually well.

A single project can have several representations:

```text
SMALL

┌───────────┐
│ LEGE      │
│ ● active  │
└───────────┘
```

```text
MEDIUM

┌────────────────────────────┐
│ LEGE                       │
│                            │
│ >_ Terminal  📁 Files  Edit│
└────────────────────────────┘
```

```text
LARGE

┌─────────────────────────────────────┐
│ LEGE                                │
│ /data/projects/Lege                 │
│                                     │
│ >_ Terminal   📁 Files   </> Edit   │
│                                     │
│ Recent                              │
│ README.md                           │
│ distro/planning/...                 │
│                                     │
│ References                          │
│ GitHub · docs · benchmark results   │
└─────────────────────────────────────┘
```

The tile doesn't become a file manager when enlarged.

It becomes a richer **project control surface**.

That distinction matters.

---

# Your `Dev` idea should probably be a standard project template

Rather than manually creating a special Dev widget, define a project presentation/template:

```text
Project type: Development
```

Not necessarily stored as a hard taxonomy; it can initially just be inferred or selected.

Its standard actions are:

```text
Terminal
Files
Editor
```

Later you could add:

```text
Build
Test
Run
Repository
Docs
```

when the project defines those actions.

For example, a Rust project could expose:

```text
Build → cargo build
Test  → cargo test
```

but I would **not automatically execute commands merely because Cargo.toml exists** until you have a proper project-action model.

The first three are universal and safe.

---

# There's a useful interaction between this and your no-polling rule

Avoid turning project tiles into little monitoring daemons.

It's tempting to put:

```text
Git branch
12 modified files
tests passing
CPU used by project
latest commit
build status
```

on every project tile.

That can recreate exactly the background-service mess you were trying to eliminate.

The rule should be:

> Project widgets may display continuously changing information only when an existing authoritative event source already provides it.

So:

```text
running instances
```

can come from session/window state.

```text
active build
```

can come from the resource/process manager.

```text
recent files
```

can come from the shared index.

Don't initially have every project tile launch its own `git status` every five seconds.

That would undermine your new system philosophy.

---

# One compromise with PCManFM needs to be understood

Stock PCManFM will not automatically use all of `lege-filed`.

It uses libfm-qt/GLib for its own file-management behavior. ([GitHub][3])

So initially:

```text
PCMan copy/move/delete
        ↓
filesystem
        ↓
Templar index notices events
```

rather than:
